[
{ch:18,q:"What defines a state of dynamic equilibrium in a closed system?",choices:["The rate of the forward reaction becomes equal to the rate of the backward reaction","The forward reaction has completely stopped","The concentrations of reactants and products become exactly equal","The backward reaction has completely stopped"],explain:"Dynamic equilibrium is reached when the rate of the forward reaction equals the rate of the backward reaction, so there is no further net change, even though both reactions are still occurring."},
{ch:18,q:"Why is a closed system required for a chemical system to reach equilibrium?",choices:["A closed system has no exchange of matter with the surroundings, so reactants and products remain available to react and recombine","A closed system always has a higher pressure than an open system","A closed system prevents any heat exchange with the surroundings","A closed system removes the need for a catalyst"],explain:"In a closed system, matter cannot escape, so reactants and products remain available to react and recombine with each other, allowing the forward and backward rates to become equal."},
{ch:18,q:"For 2HI(g) &harr; H<sub>2</sub>(g) + I<sub>2</sub>(g) started from pure HI(g), which observation indicates equilibrium has been reached?",choices:["The purple colour of the gas mixture stops changing","The gas mixture becomes completely colourless","The total pressure in the container drops to zero","All the HI(g) has been converted to H<sub>2</sub>(g) and I<sub>2</sub>(g)"],explain:"Once equilibrium is reached, [HI], [H<sub>2</sub>] and [I<sub>2</sub>] all become constant, so the purple colour due to I<sub>2</sub>(g) stops changing, even though the forward and backward reactions are still occurring."},
{ch:18,q:"The same equilibrium mixture, with the same K<sub>c</sub>, is reached for 2HI(g) &harr; H<sub>2</sub>(g) + I<sub>2</sub>(g) whether starting from pure HI, or from a mixture of H<sub>2</sub> and I<sub>2</sub>. What does this illustrate?",choices:["Equilibrium can be reached from either direction, provided the temperature and overall atom ratios are the same","The equilibrium mixture must always contain equal concentrations of HI, H<sub>2</sub> and I<sub>2</sub>","K<sub>c</sub> depends on which species was placed in the flask initially","The reaction cannot reach equilibrium if it starts from products only"],explain:"The same equilibrium mixture results under the same conditions regardless of the starting composition, since K<sub>c</sub> is fixed by the temperature alone, not by the starting point."},
{ch:18,q:"At equilibrium, the concentrations of reactants and products are constant. What does this imply about their relative sizes?",choices:["Nothing in particular &ndash; most commonly one side is present in much higher concentration than the other","They must always be numerically equal to each other","The reactant concentration must always exceed the product concentration","The product concentration must always exceed the reactant concentration"],explain:"Constant concentrations at equilibrium do not imply equal concentrations; the ratio depends on the particular reaction and conditions, captured quantitatively by the value of K<sub>c</sub>."},
{ch:18,q:"A reaction whose equilibrium mixture contains predominantly products is described as:",choices:["Lying to the right","Lying to the left","Being irreversible","Having no equilibrium constant"],explain:"By convention, an equilibrium mixture that is mostly products is said to lie to the right, and one that is mostly reactants is said to lie to the left."},
{ch:18,q:"Which macroscopic property is expected to remain constant once a chemical system reaches equilibrium at constant temperature?",choices:["The colour of the reaction mixture","The identity of the limiting reactant used initially","The total number of collisions between all particles, reactive or not","The volume of the container if it is rigid"],explain:"Macroscopic properties such as colour depend on the concentrations of the mixture, which are constant at equilibrium, so they show no further observable change."},
{ch:18,q:"Why does the magnitude of K<sub>c</sub> give no information about how fast equilibrium is reached?",choices:["Because K<sub>c</sub> describes only the composition of the equilibrium mixture, not the rate at which it is achieved","Because K<sub>c</sub> is always equal to 1 for a fast reaction","Because rate depends only on temperature, never on K<sub>c</sub>","Because fast reactions always have a small K<sub>c</sub>"],explain:"The magnitude of K<sub>c</sub> tells us about the extent (position) of equilibrium, but gives no information about the rate at which that equilibrium state is achieved."},
{ch:18,q:"What does Le Ch&acirc;telier's principle state about a system at equilibrium that is subjected to a change?",choices:["The system responds in such a way as to minimize the effect of the change","The system always shifts to favour the products, regardless of the change","The system stops reacting until the change is reversed","The system's temperature always increases in response to any change"],explain:"Le Ch&acirc;telier's principle states that a system at equilibrium responds to a disturbance by shifting in the direction that partly counteracts, or minimizes, the effect of that disturbance."},
{ch:18,q:"Applying Le Ch&acirc;telier's principle, what happens to the equilibrium position if the concentration of a reactant is increased?",choices:["The equilibrium shifts to the right, favouring the products","The equilibrium shifts to the left, favouring the reactants","The equilibrium position is unaffected, only the rate changes","The value of K<sub>c</sub> decreases"],explain:"Increasing the concentration of a reactant increases the rate of the forward reaction, so the equilibrium shifts to the right (towards products) as the system responds to partly remove the added reactant."},
{ch:18,q:"What is the effect of a concentration change on the value of K<sub>c</sub> at constant temperature?",choices:["No effect &ndash; K<sub>c</sub> remains unchanged, even though the position of equilibrium shifts","K<sub>c</sub> always increases","K<sub>c</sub> always decreases","K<sub>c</sub> becomes undefined"],explain:"Changing concentration shifts the position of equilibrium to a new set of concentrations, but the value of K<sub>c</sub> itself is unaffected as long as the temperature stays constant."},
{ch:18,q:"For CO(g) + 2H<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; CH<sub>3</sub>OH(g), which has 3 mol of gas on the left and 1 mol of gas on the right, what is the effect of increasing the pressure?",choices:["The equilibrium shifts to the right, increasing the yield of CH<sub>3</sub>OH(g)","The equilibrium shifts to the left, decreasing the yield of CH<sub>3</sub>OH(g)","The equilibrium position is unaffected, since only concentration changes matter","The value of K<sub>c</sub> decreases"],explain:"Increasing pressure favours the side with fewer gas molecules; since the product side has only 1 mol of gas compared with 3 mol on the reactant side, the equilibrium shifts right."},
{ch:18,q:"For H<sub>2</sub>(g) + I<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; 2HI(g), which has 2 mol of gas molecules on both sides, what is the effect of increasing the pressure on the position of equilibrium and on K<sub>c</sub>?",choices:["Neither the position of equilibrium nor K<sub>c</sub> is affected","The equilibrium shifts to the right and K<sub>c</sub> increases","The equilibrium shifts to the left and K<sub>c</sub> decreases","The equilibrium shifts to the right but K<sub>c</sub> stays the same"],explain:"Since the number of gas molecules is equal on both sides, a pressure change affects only the rate of reaching equilibrium, not the position of equilibrium or the value of K<sub>c</sub>."},
{ch:18,q:"For 2NO<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub>(g), &Delta;H&deg; = &minus;57 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, what is the effect of decreasing the temperature?",choices:["The equilibrium shifts to the right (favouring N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub>) and K<sub>c</sub> increases","The equilibrium shifts to the left (favouring NO<sub>2</sub>) and K<sub>c</sub> increases","The equilibrium shifts to the right and K<sub>c</sub> decreases","The equilibrium shifts to the left and K<sub>c</sub> decreases"],explain:"The forward reaction is exothermic, so decreasing the temperature favours the forward direction: the equilibrium shifts right towards N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub>, and K<sub>c</sub> increases, consistent with K<sub>c</sub> = 1300 at 273 K compared with K<sub>c</sub> = 170 at 298 K."},
{ch:18,q:"What effect does a catalyst have on the position of equilibrium and on the value of K<sub>c</sub>?",choices:["No effect on either &ndash; it only speeds up how quickly equilibrium is reached","It shifts the equilibrium towards products and increases K<sub>c</sub>","It shifts the equilibrium towards reactants and decreases K<sub>c</sub>","It increases K<sub>c</sub> but leaves the equilibrium position unchanged"],explain:"A catalyst speeds up the forward and backward reactions by the same factor, so it has no effect on the position of equilibrium or on the value of K<sub>c</sub>; it only allows equilibrium to be reached more quickly."},
{ch:18,q:"Why does a catalyst not increase the equilibrium yield of product in a reaction?",choices:["Because it speeds up the forward and backward reactions equally, so the same equilibrium mixture is eventually reached, just faster","Because catalysts are consumed before equilibrium is reached","Because catalysts only work on reactions that never reach equilibrium","Because a catalyst always favours the reverse reaction more than the forward reaction"],explain:"Since a catalyst increases the rates of the forward and backward reactions by the same factor, the equilibrium composition (and K<sub>c</sub>) is unchanged &ndash; only the time taken to reach it is reduced."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: For the general homogeneous reaction aA + bB &harr; cC + dD, which is the correct K<sub>c</sub> expression?",choices:["K<sub>c</sub> = [C]<sup>c</sup>[D]<sup>d</sup> / ([A]<sup>a</sup>[B]<sup>b</sup>)","K<sub>c</sub> = [A]<sup>a</sup>[B]<sup>b</sup> / ([C]<sup>c</sup>[D]<sup>d</sup>)","K<sub>c</sub> = [C]<sup>c</sup>[D]<sup>d</sup> &times; [A]<sup>a</sup>[B]<sup>b</sup>","K<sub>c</sub> = ([A]<sup>a</sup> + [B]<sup>b</sup>) / ([C]<sup>c</sup> + [D]<sup>d</sup>)"],explain:"The equilibrium constant expression has the concentrations of products, each raised to its coefficient, in the numerator and the concentrations of reactants, each raised to its coefficient, in the denominator."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Write the K<sub>c</sub> expression for N<sub>2</sub>(g) + 3H<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; 2NH<sub>3</sub>(g).",choices:["K<sub>c</sub> = [NH<sub>3</sub>]<sup>2</sup> / ([N<sub>2</sub>][H<sub>2</sub>]<sup>3</sup>)","K<sub>c</sub> = [N<sub>2</sub>][H<sub>2</sub>]<sup>3</sup> / [NH<sub>3</sub>]<sup>2</sup>","K<sub>c</sub> = [NH<sub>3</sub>]<sup>2</sup> / ([N<sub>2</sub>][H<sub>2</sub>])","K<sub>c</sub> = 2[NH<sub>3</sub>] / ([N<sub>2</sub>] + 3[H<sub>2</sub>])"],explain:"Products go in the numerator and reactants in the denominator, each raised to its coefficient: K<sub>c</sub> = [NH<sub>3</sub>]<sup>2</sup> / ([N<sub>2</sub>][H<sub>2</sub>]<sup>3</sup>)."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Write the K<sub>c</sub> expression for Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 4NH<sub>3</sub>(aq) &harr; [Cu(NH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>4</sub>]<sup>2+</sup>(aq).",choices:["K<sub>c</sub> = [[Cu(NH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>4</sub>]<sup>2+</sup>] / ([Cu<sup>2+</sup>][NH<sub>3</sub>]<sup>4</sup>)","K<sub>c</sub> = [Cu<sup>2+</sup>][NH<sub>3</sub>]<sup>4</sup> / [[Cu(NH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>4</sub>]<sup>2+</sup>]","K<sub>c</sub> = [Cu<sup>2+</sup>][NH<sub>3</sub>] / [[Cu(NH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>4</sub>]<sup>2+</sup>]","K<sub>c</sub> = 4[Cu<sup>2+</sup>][NH<sub>3</sub>] / [[Cu(NH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>4</sub>]<sup>2+</sup>]"],explain:"The complex ion is the sole product, with coefficient 1, and NH<sub>3</sub> has coefficient 4, giving K<sub>c</sub> = [[Cu(NH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>4</sub>]<sup>2+</sup>] / ([Cu<sup>2+</sup>][NH<sub>3</sub>]<sup>4</sup>)."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Why must only equilibrium concentrations, never initial concentrations, be substituted into the K<sub>c</sub> expression?",choices:["Because substituting any other set of concentrations gives the reaction quotient, Q, not K<sub>c</sub>","Because initial concentrations are always larger than equilibrium concentrations","Because K<sub>c</sub> only applies before any reaction has started","Because equilibrium concentrations are always equal to initial concentrations"],explain:"The equilibrium law relates K<sub>c</sub> specifically to equilibrium concentrations; substituting non-equilibrium concentrations instead gives the reaction quotient, Q."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: The K<sub>c</sub> expressions covered in this topic apply specifically to which type of reaction?",choices:["Homogeneous reactions, where reactants and products are all in the same phase","Only reactions that are exothermic","Only reactions involving exactly two reactants","Only reactions that take place in the solid state"],explain:"These K<sub>c</sub> expressions apply to homogeneous reactions, where reactants and products are all gases, all liquids, or all in solution; state symbols confirm this."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Why is K<sub>c</sub> sometimes written simply as K in other sources?",choices:["Because K<sub>c</sub> is calculated using concentrations, and the subscript 'c' stands for 'concentration'","Because K and K<sub>c</sub> are defined by completely different equations","Because K<sub>c</sub> only applies to gaseous reactions, while K applies to all reactions","Because K is the symbol used only for the reverse reaction"],explain:"K<sub>c</sub> is the equilibrium constant calculated from concentrations; the subscript 'c' distinguishes it from equilibrium constants defined using other quantities, such as partial pressure (K<sub>p</sub>), though both refer to the same equilibrium law at this level."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: How are the units of K<sub>c</sub> derived for a particular reaction?",choices:["Substitute the unit mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> for every concentration term, keeping the same powers as the K<sub>c</sub> expression, then simplify algebraically","Units of K<sub>c</sub> are always mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>, regardless of the reaction","Units of K<sub>c</sub> are always dimensionless, regardless of the reaction","Units of K<sub>c</sub> are found by adding the coefficients of all species in the equation"],explain:"The units of K<sub>c</sub> depend on the stoichiometry of the reaction and must be derived individually by substituting mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> for each concentration term, raised to the matching power, and simplifying."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: What are the units of K<sub>c</sub> for H<sub>2</sub>(g) + I<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; 2HI(g)?",choices:["No units (the powers of concentration cancel exactly)","mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup>","mol<sup>&minus;2</sup> dm<sup>6</sup>"],explain:"K<sub>c</sub> = [HI]<sup>2</sup> / ([H<sub>2</sub>][I<sub>2</sub>]) has (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>)<sup>2</sup> in the numerator and (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>)<sup>2</sup> in the denominator, so the units cancel exactly, leaving no units."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: What are the units of K<sub>c</sub> for N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub>(g) &harr; 2NO<sub>2</sub>(g)?",choices:["mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","No units","mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup>","mol<sup>&minus;2</sup> dm<sup>6</sup>"],explain:"K<sub>c</sub> = [NO<sub>2</sub>]<sup>2</sup> / [N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub>] has (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>)<sup>2</sup> over (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>)<sup>1</sup>, leaving one remaining power of mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: What are the units of K<sub>c</sub> for 2SO<sub>2</sub>(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; 2SO<sub>3</sub>(g)?",choices:["mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup>","mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","No units","mol<sup>&minus;2</sup> dm<sup>6</sup>"],explain:"K<sub>c</sub> = [SO<sub>3</sub>]<sup>2</sup> / ([SO<sub>2</sub>]<sup>2</sup>[O<sub>2</sub>]) has (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>)<sup>2</sup> over (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>)<sup>3</sup>, leaving mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup>."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: What are the units of K<sub>c</sub> for N<sub>2</sub>(g) + 3H<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; 2NH<sub>3</sub>(g)?",choices:["mol<sup>&minus;2</sup> dm<sup>6</sup>","mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","No units","mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup>"],explain:"K<sub>c</sub> = [NH<sub>3</sub>]<sup>2</sup> / ([N<sub>2</sub>][H<sub>2</sub>]<sup>3</sup>) has (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>)<sup>2</sup> over (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>)<sup>4</sup>, leaving (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>)<sup>&minus;2</sup>, equivalent to mol<sup>&minus;2</sup> dm<sup>6</sup>."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: What general rule links the units of K<sub>c</sub> to the stoichiometry of a reaction?",choices:["The overall power of concentration in K<sub>c</sub> equals the sum of product coefficients minus the sum of reactant coefficients","The units of K<sub>c</sub> are always equal to the number of reactants in the equation","The units of K<sub>c</sub> depend only on whether the reaction is exothermic or endothermic","The units of K<sub>c</sub> can never be a negative power of mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>"],explain:"The net power of concentration (and hence the units) in K<sub>c</sub> equals (sum of product coefficients) minus (sum of reactant coefficients); when this is zero, K<sub>c</sub> has no units."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Why do some sources state that K<sub>c</sub> has no units under any circumstances, despite the derivations above?",choices:["Because strictly the terms in the equilibrium expression represent a dimensionless thermodynamic quantity called activity","Because concentration itself has no units in mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","Because K<sub>c</sub> is only ever measured for gaseous reactions","Because the data booklet does not provide units for any equilibrium constant"],explain:"Strictly, the terms in the equilibrium expression represent activity, a dimensionless thermodynamic quantity, so some sources and exam markschemes accept K<sub>c</sub> without units, even though units can still be derived as shown."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: A reaction has K<sub>c</sub> &#8811; 1 at a given temperature. What does this indicate?",choices:["The reaction goes almost to completion, with the equilibrium mixture lying strongly towards products","The reaction hardly proceeds, with the equilibrium mixture lying strongly towards reactants","The reaction has exactly equal amounts of reactants and products at equilibrium","The reaction is proceeding very slowly, regardless of composition"],explain:"K<sub>c</sub> &#8811; 1 means products dominate hugely in the numerator, so the equilibrium mixture lies far towards products and the reaction goes almost to completion."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: A reaction has K<sub>c</sub> &#8810; 1 at a given temperature. What does this indicate?",choices:["The reaction hardly proceeds, with the equilibrium mixture lying strongly towards reactants","The reaction goes almost to completion","The reaction proceeds extremely quickly","The value of K<sub>c</sub> will increase if the temperature is kept constant"],explain:"K<sub>c</sub> &#8810; 1 means the denominator (reactants) dominates hugely, so the equilibrium mixture lies far towards reactants and the reaction has barely taken place."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: At 550 K, H<sub>2</sub>(g) + I<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; 2HI(g) has K<sub>c</sub> = 2, while H<sub>2</sub>(g) + Cl<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; 2HCl(g) has K<sub>c</sub> = 10<sup>18</sup>. What can be concluded?",choices:["At this temperature, the reaction of H<sub>2</sub> with Cl<sub>2</sub> has proceeded much more fully than the reaction of H<sub>2</sub> with I<sub>2</sub>","The reaction of H<sub>2</sub> with I<sub>2</sub> occurs faster than the reaction of H<sub>2</sub> with Cl<sub>2</sub>","Both reactions have identical equilibrium positions","The reaction of H<sub>2</sub> with Cl<sub>2</sub> cannot be at equilibrium since its K<sub>c</sub> value is so large"],explain:"A far larger K<sub>c</sub> for the H<sub>2</sub> + Cl<sub>2</sub> reaction shows its equilibrium mixture lies much further towards products than the H<sub>2</sub> + I<sub>2</sub> reaction; K<sub>c</sub> says nothing about reaction rate."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: At 550 K, H<sub>2</sub>(g) + I<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; 2HI(g) has K<sub>c</sub> = 2. What does this value indicate about the equilibrium mixture?",choices:["Appreciable, comparable amounts of both HI and H<sub>2</sub>/I<sub>2</sub> are present at equilibrium","The reaction has gone essentially to completion","The reaction mixture consists almost entirely of H<sub>2</sub> and I<sub>2</sub>","No HI(g) is present at equilibrium"],explain:"A K<sub>c</sub> value close to 1 (here, 2) indicates that significant, comparable amounts of both reactants and products are present at equilibrium."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: For aA + bB &harr; cC + dD with equilibrium constant K<sub>c</sub>, what is K<sub>c</sub>&prime; for the reverse reaction cC + dD &harr; aA + bB?",choices:["K<sub>c</sub>&prime; = 1 / K<sub>c</sub>","K<sub>c</sub>&prime; = K<sub>c</sub>","K<sub>c</sub>&prime; = &minus;K<sub>c</sub>","K<sub>c</sub>&prime; = K<sub>c</sub> + 1"],explain:"Reversing a reaction swaps the numerator and denominator of the equilibrium expression, so K<sub>c</sub>&prime; = 1 / K<sub>c</sub>."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: K<sub>c</sub> = 0.040 for 2HI(g) &harr; H<sub>2</sub>(g) + I<sub>2</sub>(g) at a certain temperature. What is K<sub>c</sub>&prime; for H<sub>2</sub>(g) + I<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; 2HI(g) at the same temperature?",choices:["25","0.040","0.0016","40"],explain:"K<sub>c</sub>&prime; = 1 / K<sub>c</sub> = 1 / 0.040 = 25."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: If every coefficient in an equation is doubled, how does the new equilibrium constant relate to the original K<sub>c</sub>?",choices:["The new equilibrium constant is K<sub>c</sub><sup>2</sup>","The new equilibrium constant is K<sub>c</sub> / 2","The new equilibrium constant is &radic;K<sub>c</sub>","The new equilibrium constant is unchanged"],explain:"Doubling every coefficient in a reaction equation squares the equilibrium constant, since each concentration term's exponent is doubled."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Which single statement best distinguishes the effect of concentration and pressure changes from the effect of a temperature change on an equilibrium system?",choices:["Concentration and pressure changes can shift the equilibrium position but never change K<sub>c</sub>, whereas a temperature change does both","Concentration and pressure changes always change K<sub>c</sub>, whereas temperature changes never do","Only temperature changes can shift the equilibrium position; concentration and pressure cannot","None of these three factors can change the equilibrium position, only K<sub>c</sub>"],explain:"Concentration and pressure changes shift the position of equilibrium but leave K<sub>c</sub> unchanged at constant temperature; a temperature change is unique in changing both the position of equilibrium and the value of K<sub>c</sub> itself."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Why does a temperature change, unlike concentration or pressure changes, alter the actual value of K<sub>c</sub>?",choices:["Because temperature affects the rate constants of the forward and backward reactions by different amounts, owing to their different activation energies","Because temperature always increases both rate constants by exactly the same factor","Because concentration and pressure changes always produce a larger effect on rate than temperature does","Because K<sub>c</sub> is defined only in terms of temperature, never in terms of concentration"],explain:"A temperature change alters the rate constants of the forward and backward reactions unequally, since they generally have different activation energies, so the ratio of rates (and hence K<sub>c</sub>) genuinely changes."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: For 2NO<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub>(g), &Delta;H&deg; = &minus;57 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, K<sub>c</sub> = 1300 at 273 K but only 170 at 298 K. What does this show?",choices:["For an exothermic forward reaction, K<sub>c</sub> decreases as temperature increases","For an exothermic forward reaction, K<sub>c</sub> increases as temperature increases","K<sub>c</sub> is unaffected by temperature for this reaction","The reaction is endothermic, not exothermic, based on this data"],explain:"The forward reaction is exothermic (&Delta;H&deg; negative); increasing the temperature from 273 K to 298 K shifts the equilibrium in the endothermic (backward) direction, decreasing K<sub>c</sub> from 1300 to 170."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: For N<sub>2</sub>(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; 2NO(g), &Delta;H&deg; = +181 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, K<sub>c</sub> rises from 4.5 &times; 10<sup>&minus;31</sup> at 298 K to 1.7 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> at 2300 K. What does this show?",choices:["For an endothermic forward reaction, K<sub>c</sub> increases as temperature increases","For an endothermic forward reaction, K<sub>c</sub> decreases as temperature increases","K<sub>c</sub> is unaffected by temperature for this reaction","The reaction is exothermic, not endothermic, based on this data"],explain:"The forward reaction is endothermic (&Delta;H&deg; positive); increasing the temperature favours the forward direction, so K<sub>c</sub> increases dramatically as temperature rises."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: For an exothermic forward reaction, what happens to K<sub>c</sub> as temperature increases?",choices:["K<sub>c</sub> decreases","K<sub>c</sub> increases","K<sub>c</sub> stays constant","K<sub>c</sub> becomes negative"],explain:"Increasing temperature favours the endothermic (backward) direction of an exothermic forward reaction, shifting equilibrium left and decreasing K<sub>c</sub>."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: For an endothermic forward reaction, what happens to K<sub>c</sub> as temperature increases?",choices:["K<sub>c</sub> increases","K<sub>c</sub> decreases","K<sub>c</sub> stays constant","K<sub>c</sub> becomes undefined"],explain:"Increasing temperature favours the endothermic (forward) direction, shifting equilibrium right and increasing K<sub>c</sub>."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Which of the four factors in Le Ch&acirc;telier's principle &ndash; concentration, pressure, temperature, catalyst &ndash; is the only one that changes the actual value of K<sub>c</sub>?",choices:["Temperature","Concentration","Pressure","Catalyst"],explain:"Concentration, pressure and catalyst changes can shift the position of equilibrium, but only a temperature change alters the value of K<sub>c</sub> itself."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: At equilibrium at 500&deg;C, a mixture for CO(g) + H<sub>2</sub>O(g) &harr; H<sub>2</sub>(g) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g) has [CO] = 0.150, [H<sub>2</sub>O] = 0.0145, [H<sub>2</sub>] = 0.200 and [CO<sub>2</sub>] = 0.0200 (all mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>). Calculate K<sub>c</sub>.",choices:["1.84","0.543","0.00400","2.00"],explain:"K<sub>c</sub> = ([H<sub>2</sub>][CO<sub>2</sub>]) / ([CO][H<sub>2</sub>O]) = (0.200 &times; 0.0200) / (0.150 &times; 0.0145) = 0.00400 / 0.002175 = 1.84 (no units, since the powers cancel)."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: 0.20 mol of PCl<sub>3</sub>(g) and 0.10 mol of Cl<sub>2</sub>(g) are placed in a 1 dm<sup>3</sup> flask at 350&deg;C. At equilibrium, PCl<sub>3</sub>(g) + Cl<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; PCl<sub>5</sub>(g) has produced 0.12 mol of PCl<sub>5</sub>. What is the equilibrium concentration of Cl<sub>2</sub>?",choices:["0.02 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","0.10 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","0.08 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","0.12 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>"],explain:"The change in Cl<sub>2</sub> equals the change in PCl<sub>5</sub> (1:1 stoichiometry), so change = &minus;0.08, giving [Cl<sub>2</sub>]<sub>eqm</sub> = 0.10 &minus; 0.08 = 0.02 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: For the same reaction, PCl<sub>3</sub>(g) + Cl<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; PCl<sub>5</sub>(g), with equilibrium concentrations [PCl<sub>3</sub>] = 0.12, [Cl<sub>2</sub>] = 0.02 and [PCl<sub>5</sub>] = 0.08 (all mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>), calculate K<sub>c</sub>.",choices:["33.3 mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup>","0.0300 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","0.667 mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup>","4.00 mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup>"],explain:"K<sub>c</sub> = [PCl<sub>5</sub>] / ([PCl<sub>3</sub>][Cl<sub>2</sub>]) = 0.08 / (0.12 &times; 0.02) = 0.08 / 0.0024 = 33.3 mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup>."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: 0.60 mol of NO(g) and 0.60 mol of O<sub>2</sub>(g) react in a 2 dm<sup>3</sup> container at 500&deg;C: 2NO(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; 2NO<sub>2</sub>(g). Why must the initial amounts in mol be divided by 2 before use in an ICE table?",choices:["Because concentration (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>) is required for the K<sub>c</sub> expression, and the container volume is 2 dm<sup>3</sup>, not 1 dm<sup>3</sup>","Because the reaction only occurs when amounts are halved first","Because K<sub>c</sub> always requires amounts to be divided by the number of species in the equation","Because volume has no effect on equilibrium concentration calculations"],explain:"The K<sub>c</sub> expression requires concentration in mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>; since the container is 2 dm<sup>3</sup>, every amount in mol must be divided by 2 to convert it to a concentration."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: For 2NO(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; 2NO<sub>2</sub>(g) in a 2 dm<sup>3</sup> container, starting from 0.60 mol NO and 0.60 mol O<sub>2</sub>, equilibrium contains 0.20 mol NO<sub>2</sub>. What is the equilibrium concentration of NO?",choices:["0.20 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","0.30 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","0.10 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","0.25 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>"],explain:"Initial [NO] = 0.60/2 = 0.30 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>; [NO<sub>2</sub>]<sub>eqm</sub> = 0.20/2 = 0.10 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>, so change in [NO] = &minus;0.10 (1:1 ratio with NO<sub>2</sub>), giving [NO]<sub>eqm</sub> = 0.30 &minus; 0.10 = 0.20 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: For the same reaction, with equilibrium concentrations [NO] = 0.20, [O<sub>2</sub>] = 0.25 and [NO<sub>2</sub>] = 0.10 (all mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>), calculate K<sub>c</sub>.",choices:["1.0 mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup>","4.0 mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup>","0.25 mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup>","0.010 mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup>"],explain:"K<sub>c</sub> = [NO<sub>2</sub>]<sup>2</sup> / ([NO]<sup>2</sup>[O<sub>2</sub>]) = (0.10)<sup>2</sup> / ((0.20)<sup>2</sup> &times; 0.25) = 0.0100 / 0.0100 = 1.0 mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup>."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: CO(g) + 2H<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; CH<sub>3</sub>OH(g) has K<sub>c</sub> = 0.500 at 350 K. At equilibrium, [CO] = 0.200 and [H<sub>2</sub>] = 0.155 (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>). Calculate [CH<sub>3</sub>OH] at equilibrium.",choices:["2.40 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","0.500 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","0.0310 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","1.55 &times; 10<sup>&minus;2</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>"],explain:"K<sub>c</sub> = [CH<sub>3</sub>OH] / ([CO][H<sub>2</sub>]<sup>2</sup>), so [CH<sub>3</sub>OH] = 0.500 &times; 0.200 &times; (0.155)<sup>2</sup> = 0.500 &times; 0.200 &times; 0.024025 = 0.00240 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: SO<sub>2</sub>(g) + NO(g) &harr; NO<sub>2</sub>(g) + SO<sub>3</sub>(g) has K<sub>c</sub> = 6.78. Both NO and SO<sub>2</sub> start at 0.0300 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>, with no products initially. Why can the square root of both sides of the K<sub>c</sub> equation be taken directly in this case?",choices:["Because the two reactant terms in the denominator are identical, (0.0300 &minus; x), making both sides of the equation a perfect square","Because K<sub>c</sub> is less than 10, which always allows a square root shortcut","Because the reaction only has one product","Because the stoichiometric coefficients are all equal to 2"],explain:"Since both reactants start at the same concentration and react in a 1:1:1:1 ratio, the K<sub>c</sub> expression becomes x<sup>2</sup> / (0.0300 &minus; x)<sup>2</sup>, a perfect square on both sides, so the square root can be taken directly."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: For SO<sub>2</sub>(g) + NO(g) &harr; NO<sub>2</sub>(g) + SO<sub>3</sub>(g), K<sub>c</sub> = 6.78, with both reactants starting at 0.0300 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>, solving x / (0.0300 &minus; x) = &radic;6.78 gives which value of x?",choices:["0.0217 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","0.0300 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","0.0083 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","0.0600 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>"],explain:"&radic;6.78 = 2.60, so x = 2.60(0.0300 &minus; x), giving x(1 + 2.60) = 0.0780 and x = 0.0780/3.60 = 0.0217 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Using x = 0.0217 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> for SO<sub>2</sub>(g) + NO(g) &harr; NO<sub>2</sub>(g) + SO<sub>3</sub>(g) (initial [SO<sub>2</sub>] = [NO] = 0.0300 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>), what is the equilibrium concentration of SO<sub>2</sub>?",choices:["0.00830 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","0.0217 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","0.0300 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","0.0517 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>"],explain:"[SO<sub>2</sub>]<sub>eqm</sub> = 0.0300 &minus; x = 0.0300 &minus; 0.0217 = 0.00830 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: H<sub>2</sub>(g) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; H<sub>2</sub>O(g) + CO(g) has K<sub>c</sub> = 0.640. 0.300 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> of H<sub>2</sub> is mixed with 0.400 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> of CO<sub>2</sub>, with no products initially. Why does this ICE table require the full quadratic formula rather than a square-root shortcut?",choices:["Because the two reactants have different initial concentrations, so the two bracketed terms in the denominator are not identical","Because K<sub>c</sub> is less than 1 for this reaction","Because the products have different coefficients from the reactants","Because this reaction involves four different species"],explain:"Since [H<sub>2</sub>]<sub>initial</sub> &ne; [CO<sub>2</sub>]<sub>initial</sub>, the denominator (0.300 &minus; x)(0.400 &minus; x) is not a perfect square, so the quadratic must be expanded and solved using the quadratic formula."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: For H<sub>2</sub>(g) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; H<sub>2</sub>O(g) + CO(g), K<sub>c</sub> = 0.640, with initial [H<sub>2</sub>] = 0.300 and [CO<sub>2</sub>] = 0.400 (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>), expanding K<sub>c</sub> = x<sup>2</sup> / [(0.300&minus;x)(0.400&minus;x)] gives which quadratic equation in standard form?",choices:["0.360x<sup>2</sup> + 0.448x &minus; 0.0768 = 0","x<sup>2</sup> &minus; 0.700x + 0.120 = 0","0.640x<sup>2</sup> &minus; 0.448x + 0.0768 = 0","x<sup>2</sup> + 0.640x &minus; 0.120 = 0"],explain:"Expanding (0.300&minus;x)(0.400&minus;x) = 0.120 &minus; 0.700x + x<sup>2</sup> and setting x<sup>2</sup> = 0.640(0.120 &minus; 0.700x + x<sup>2</sup>) rearranges to 0.360x<sup>2</sup> + 0.448x &minus; 0.0768 = 0."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Solving 0.360x<sup>2</sup> + 0.448x &minus; 0.0768 = 0 using the quadratic formula gives two roots, x = 0.153 and x = &minus;1.40 (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>). Which root is physically valid, and why?",choices:["x = 0.153, because a negative concentration change is not physically possible here","x = &minus;1.40, because K<sub>c</sub> is positive","Both roots are equally valid chemically","Neither root is valid; the equation must be solved differently"],explain:"Only x = 0.153 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> gives physically sensible equilibrium concentrations (all positive and less than the initial values); the negative root, x = &minus;1.40, is rejected as chemically impossible."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Using x = 0.153 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> for H<sub>2</sub>(g) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; H<sub>2</sub>O(g) + CO(g) (initial [H<sub>2</sub>] = 0.300, [CO<sub>2</sub>] = 0.400 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>), what is the equilibrium concentration of CO<sub>2</sub>?",choices:["0.247 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","0.147 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","0.153 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","0.400 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>"],explain:"[CO<sub>2</sub>]<sub>eqm</sub> = 0.400 &minus; x = 0.400 &minus; 0.153 = 0.247 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: At 1000&deg;C, K<sub>c</sub> = 7.3 &times; 10<sup>&minus;18</sup> for 2H<sub>2</sub>O(g) &harr; 2H<sub>2</sub>(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g). Why is the approximation [H<sub>2</sub>O]<sub>initial</sub> &asymp; [H<sub>2</sub>O]<sub>equilibrium</sub> justified here?",choices:["Because K<sub>c</sub> is so small that the forward reaction proceeds hardly at all, making the change in [H<sub>2</sub>O] negligible compared with its initial value","Because water always has a constant concentration in any reaction","Because this reaction only occurs at very high temperature","Because the stoichiometric coefficients of H<sub>2</sub>O and H<sub>2</sub> are equal"],explain:"A rule of thumb is that when K<sub>c</sub> &lt; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup>, the forward reaction proceeds so little that the change in a reactant's concentration is negligible, justifying [reactant]<sub>initial</sub> &asymp; [reactant]<sub>equilibrium</sub>."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: For 2H<sub>2</sub>O(g) &harr; 2H<sub>2</sub>(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g), K<sub>c</sub> = 7.3 &times; 10<sup>&minus;18</sup> at 1000&deg;C, with initial [H<sub>2</sub>O] = 0.10 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>, letting the change in [H<sub>2</sub>] = 2x and in [O<sub>2</sub>] = x gives which equation for K<sub>c</sub>?",choices:["K<sub>c</sub> = (2x)<sup>2</sup>(x) / (0.10)<sup>2</sup>","K<sub>c</sub> = (2x)<sup>2</sup>(x) / (0.10 &minus; 2x)<sup>2</sup>","K<sub>c</sub> = x<sup>2</sup> / (0.10)<sup>2</sup>","K<sub>c</sub> = (2x) / (0.10)<sup>2</sup>"],explain:"Using the approximation [H<sub>2</sub>O]<sub>eqm</sub> &asymp; 0.10, K<sub>c</sub> = [H<sub>2</sub>]<sup>2</sup>[O<sub>2</sub>] / [H<sub>2</sub>O]<sup>2</sup> = (2x)<sup>2</sup>(x) / (0.10)<sup>2</sup>."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Solving 4x<sup>3</sup> = 7.3 &times; 10<sup>&minus;20</sup> for the H<sub>2</sub>O decomposition example gives x = 2.6 &times; 10<sup>&minus;7</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>. What is the equilibrium concentration of H<sub>2</sub>?",choices:["5.3 &times; 10<sup>&minus;7</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","2.6 &times; 10<sup>&minus;7</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","1.3 &times; 10<sup>&minus;7</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","2.6 &times; 10<sup>&minus;14</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>"],explain:"Since the change in [H<sub>2</sub>] was defined as 2x, [H<sub>2</sub>]<sub>eqm</sub> = 2x = 2 &times; 2.6 &times; 10<sup>&minus;7</sup> = 5.3 &times; 10<sup>&minus;7</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: As a rule of thumb, for what range of K<sub>c</sub> values is the approximation [reactant]<sub>initial</sub> &asymp; [reactant]<sub>equilibrium</sub> generally justified?",choices:["K<sub>c</sub> &lt; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup>","K<sub>c</sub> &gt; 10<sup>5</sup>","K<sub>c</sub> between 0.1 and 10","K<sub>c</sub> exactly equal to 1"],explain:"When K<sub>c</sub> is less than about 10<sup>&minus;5</sup>, the reaction proceeds so little that the change in reactant concentration is negligible compared with its initial value, justifying the approximation."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Why is this small-K<sub>c</sub> approximation especially common in the study of weak acids and bases?",choices:["Because weak acids and bases dissociate only slightly in solution, giving a very small K<sub>a</sub> or K<sub>b</sub> value","Because weak acids and bases always have K<sub>c</sub> greater than 1","Because strong acids cannot be studied using equilibrium methods","Because weak acids never reach equilibrium"],explain:"Weak acids and bases dissociate only slightly, giving very small equilibrium constants (K<sub>a</sub> or K<sub>b</sub>), so the approximation [acid]<sub>initial</sub> &asymp; [acid]<sub>equilibrium</sub> is commonly used to determine pH and pOH."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: When solving a quadratic that arises from an ICE table, a student finds two mathematical roots. How should the correct root be chosen?",choices:["Discard any root that is negative, or that would make an equilibrium concentration negative or exceed the amount available initially","Always choose the larger of the two roots","Always choose the smaller of the two roots","Average the two roots together"],explain:"A valid root for a change in concentration must keep every equilibrium concentration between zero and its initial value; the other mathematically valid root is always rejected as chemically impossible."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: The reaction quotient, Q, is calculated using which concentrations?",choices:["Concentrations of reactants and products measured at any point in time, not necessarily at equilibrium","Only the initial concentrations placed in the reaction vessel","Only the equilibrium concentrations, exactly as for K<sub>c</sub>","Only the concentrations of the products, never the reactants"],explain:"Q is calculated using the same expression as K<sub>c</sub>, but substituting concentrations measured at any particular moment in time, which need not be the equilibrium concentrations."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: What does it mean if Q &lt; K<sub>c</sub> for a reaction at a given moment?",choices:["The reaction will proceed to the right (forward), forming more products, until Q rises to equal K<sub>c</sub>","The reaction is already at equilibrium","The reaction will proceed to the left (backward), forming more reactants","The reaction cannot proceed any further in either direction"],explain:"If Q is less than K<sub>c</sub>, there is proportionally too little product for equilibrium, so the reaction proceeds forward (to the right) until Q rises to equal K<sub>c</sub>."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: What does it mean if Q &gt; K<sub>c</sub> for a reaction at a given moment?",choices:["The reaction will proceed to the left (backward), forming more reactants, until Q falls to equal K<sub>c</sub>","The reaction will proceed to the right, forming more products","The reaction is already at equilibrium","K<sub>c</sub> itself will increase to match Q"],explain:"If Q is greater than K<sub>c</sub>, there is proportionally too much product for equilibrium, so the reaction proceeds backward (to the left) until Q falls to equal K<sub>c</sub>."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: What does Q = K<sub>c</sub> indicate about a reaction mixture?",choices:["The mixture is already at equilibrium, with no net reaction occurring","The reaction is proceeding rapidly to the right","The reaction is proceeding rapidly to the left","The temperature of the system is about to change"],explain:"When Q equals K<sub>c</sub>, the current composition already matches the equilibrium composition, so there is no net reaction in either direction."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: K<sub>c</sub> = 1.7 &times; 10<sup>2</sup> at 500 K for N<sub>2</sub>(g) + 3H<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; 2NH<sub>3</sub>(g). At a particular moment, [N<sub>2</sub>] = 1.50, [H<sub>2</sub>] = 1.00, [NH<sub>3</sub>] = 8.00 (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>). Calculate Q.",choices:["42.7","170","64.0","1.50"],explain:"Q = [NH<sub>3</sub>]<sup>2</sup> / ([N<sub>2</sub>][H<sub>2</sub>]<sup>3</sup>) = (8.00)<sup>2</sup> / (1.50 &times; (1.00)<sup>3</sup>) = 64.0 / 1.50 = 42.7."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Given Q = 42.7 and K<sub>c</sub> = 1.7 &times; 10<sup>2</sup> for N<sub>2</sub>(g) + 3H<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; 2NH<sub>3</sub>(g), in which direction will the reaction proceed?",choices:["To the right, forming more NH<sub>3</sub>, since Q &lt; K<sub>c</sub>","To the left, forming more N<sub>2</sub> and H<sub>2</sub>, since Q &gt; K<sub>c</sub>","No net reaction, since Q = K<sub>c</sub>","It is impossible to determine the direction from this data"],explain:"Since Q (42.7) is less than K<sub>c</sub> (170), the reaction mixture is not yet at equilibrium and will proceed to the right, forming more NH<sub>3</sub>, until Q rises to 170."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: K<sub>c</sub> = 1.00 for CO(g) + H<sub>2</sub>O(g) &harr; CO<sub>2</sub>(g) + H<sub>2</sub>(g) at 298 K. At a given moment, [CO] = 2.00, [H<sub>2</sub>O] = 2.00, [CO<sub>2</sub>] = 4.00, [H<sub>2</sub>] = 4.00 (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>). Calculate Q.",choices:["4.00","1.00","16.0","0.250"],explain:"Q = ([CO<sub>2</sub>][H<sub>2</sub>]) / ([CO][H<sub>2</sub>O]) = (4.00 &times; 4.00) / (2.00 &times; 2.00) = 16.0 / 4.00 = 4.00."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Given Q = 4.00 and K<sub>c</sub> = 1.00 for CO(g) + H<sub>2</sub>O(g) &harr; CO<sub>2</sub>(g) + H<sub>2</sub>(g), in which direction will the reaction proceed?",choices:["To the left, converting some CO<sub>2</sub> and H<sub>2</sub> back into CO and H<sub>2</sub>O","To the right, forming more CO<sub>2</sub> and H<sub>2</sub>","No net reaction, since the mixture is already at equilibrium","The reaction will stop entirely, since Q exceeds K<sub>c</sub>"],explain:"Since Q (4.00) is greater than K<sub>c</sub> (1.00), there is proportionally too much product, so the reaction proceeds to the left until Q falls to 1.00."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Why does the value of Q generally differ from one moment to the next during a reaction, unlike K<sub>c</sub>?",choices:["Because Q is calculated from concentrations that are still changing as the reaction proceeds, whereas K<sub>c</sub> has one fixed value at a given temperature","Because Q depends on temperature in a completely different way from K<sub>c</sub>","Because Q can only be measured once, at the very start of a reaction","Because Q and K<sub>c</sub> are never related to each other"],explain:"Q tracks the current, changing composition of a reaction mixture at any moment, so its value shifts as the reaction proceeds, whereas K<sub>c</sub> is fixed for a given reaction at a given temperature."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Which equation correctly relates the standard Gibbs energy change, &Delta;G&deg;, to the equilibrium constant K?",choices:["&Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K","&Delta;G&deg; = RT ln K","&Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT / ln K","&Delta;G&deg; = R ln K / T"],explain:"The relationship between standard Gibbs energy change and the equilibrium constant is &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K, where R is the gas constant and T is the absolute temperature."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: In the equation &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K, what does R represent?",choices:["The gas constant, 8.31 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","The rate constant of the forward reaction","The universal reaction quotient","The radius of the reacting molecules"],explain:"R is the gas constant, with a value of 8.31 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, used in this equation together with the absolute temperature, T, in kelvin."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Why must care be taken with units when using &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K?",choices:["Because &Delta;G&deg; is often quoted in kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> while R has units of J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, so one value must be converted to match","Because ln K always has units of J mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","Because R changes value depending on the reaction","Because T must always be converted from kelvin to degrees Celsius first"],explain:"Since &Delta;G&deg; is commonly given in kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> and R in J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, either &Delta;G&deg; must be converted to J mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> or R to kJ K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> before the equation is used."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: What value of K corresponds to a large negative &Delta;G&deg;?",choices:["K &#8811; 1, with an equilibrium mixture dominated by products","K &#8810; 1, with an equilibrium mixture dominated by reactants","K = 1 exactly","K = 0"],explain:"A large negative &Delta;G&deg; gives a large positive value of ln K (since ln K = &minus;&Delta;G&deg;/RT), and hence a large value of K, corresponding to an equilibrium mixture dominated by products."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: What value of K corresponds to a large positive &Delta;G&deg;?",choices:["K &#8810; 1, with an equilibrium mixture dominated by reactants","K &#8811; 1, with an equilibrium mixture dominated by products","K = 1 exactly","K becomes negative"],explain:"A large positive &Delta;G&deg; gives a large negative value of ln K, and hence a very small value of K, corresponding to an equilibrium mixture dominated by reactants."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: What value of K corresponds to &Delta;G&deg; = 0?",choices:["K = 1","K &#8811; 1","K &#8810; 1","K is undefined"],explain:"When &Delta;G&deg; = 0, ln K = 0, so K = 1, corresponding to appreciable, comparable amounts of both reactants and products at equilibrium."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: The esterification reaction CH<sub>3</sub>COOH(aq) + C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH(aq) &harr; CH<sub>3</sub>COOC<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(aq) has &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;4.38 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> at 298 K. Calculate ln K.",choices:["1.77","&minus;1.77","4380","0.00177"],explain:"ln K = &minus;&Delta;G&deg; / (RT) = 4380 / (8.31 &times; 298) = 4380 / 2476 = 1.77 (using &Delta;G&deg; converted to +4380 J mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> when solving for &minus;&Delta;G&deg;)."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Using ln K = 1.77 for the esterification reaction, calculate K<sub>c</sub> at 298 K.",choices:["5.86","1.77","0.569","7.63"],explain:"K = e<sup>1.77</sup> = 5.86. Since K is only a little above 1, the equilibrium mixture contains appreciable, comparable amounts of both reactants and products, consistent with &Delta;G&deg; being only modestly negative."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: The ionic product of water, K<sub>w</sub> = 1.00 &times; 10<sup>&minus;14</sup> at 298 K, for H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &harr; H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + OH<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq). What is the approximate sign and order of magnitude of &Delta;G&deg; for this equilibrium?",choices:["Large and positive, around +80 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","Large and negative, around &minus;80 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","Close to zero, around 0 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","Large and positive, around +800 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"&Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K = &minus;(8.31)(298) ln(1.00 &times; 10<sup>&minus;14</sup>) = &minus;(2476)(&minus;32.2) = +79 800 J mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> &asymp; +79.8 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, consistent with the very small value of K<sub>w</sub>."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Why is &Delta;G&deg; for the autoionization of water, H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &harr; H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + OH<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq), large and positive?",choices:["Because the equilibrium constant, K<sub>w</sub> = 1.00 &times; 10<sup>&minus;14</sup>, is extremely small, meaning the reaction barely proceeds and the mixture is almost entirely H<sub>2</sub>O(l)","Because water molecules cannot form ions under any conditions","Because this reaction is always at equilibrium regardless of composition","Because &Delta;G&deg; is always positive for any reaction producing ions"],explain:"A very small K (here, 1.00 &times; 10<sup>&minus;14</sup>) corresponds, through &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K, to a large positive &Delta;G&deg;, consistent with the reaction proceeding to only a tiny extent."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: What is the key difference between the standard Gibbs energy change, &Delta;G&deg;, used in &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K, and the instantaneous Gibbs energy change, &Delta;G, of a reaction mixture at some arbitrary composition?",choices:["&Delta;G&deg; relates to the fixed equilibrium constant, K, at a given temperature, while instantaneous &Delta;G depends on Q and becomes zero only once the mixture actually reaches equilibrium","&Delta;G&deg; and instantaneous &Delta;G are identical in every situation","&Delta;G&deg; only applies to gaseous reactions, while instantaneous &Delta;G applies to all reactions","&Delta;G&deg; is always positive, while instantaneous &Delta;G is always negative"],explain:"&Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K uses standard states and the single, fixed equilibrium constant at a given temperature, whereas the instantaneous &Delta;G of a non-equilibrium mixture depends on the reaction quotient Q, becoming zero only when the mixture reaches equilibrium."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: As a reaction proceeds towards equilibrium from either pure reactants or pure products, what happens to the total Gibbs energy of the system?",choices:["It decreases towards a minimum value, which defines the equilibrium composition","It increases towards a maximum value","It remains constant throughout","It oscillates repeatedly between high and low values"],explain:"The total Gibbs energy of the system falls as the reaction proceeds, whether starting from pure reactants or pure products, until it reaches a minimum value that corresponds to the equilibrium composition."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: On a graph of total Gibbs energy against the extent of reaction, what characterizes the equilibrium composition?",choices:["It is the single minimum point of the curve, where the slope (and &Delta;G&deg;) is zero","It is the highest point of the curve, where the slope is zero","It is always located exactly halfway between pure reactants and pure products","It is the point where the curve crosses the horizontal axis"],explain:"Equilibrium corresponds to the minimum of the Gibbs energy curve, where the slope of the curve (and therefore &Delta;G&deg;) is zero; to the left of this minimum &Delta;G&deg; is negative (forward reaction spontaneous), and to the right it is positive (backward reaction spontaneous)."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: If the minimum of the Gibbs energy curve lies far towards the products side, what does this imply about K?",choices:["K is large, and the equilibrium mixture is mostly products","K is small, and the equilibrium mixture is mostly reactants","K = 1 exactly, regardless of the minimum's position","K cannot be determined from the position of the minimum"],explain:"If the Gibbs energy minimum lies close to pure products, the equilibrium mixture is dominated by products, which corresponds to a large value of K."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Which statement correctly links &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K to the content of Reactivity 1.4 on Gibbs energy and spontaneity?",choices:["A reaction with a large negative &Delta;G&deg; (strongly spontaneous in the forward direction) corresponds to a large value of K, since both describe the same strongly product-favoured equilibrium","A reaction with a large negative &Delta;G&deg; always corresponds to a very small value of K","&Delta;G&deg; and K are entirely unrelated quantities describing different reactions","A reaction can have a large negative &Delta;G&deg; and a K value close to zero at the same temperature"],explain:"Since &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K, a large negative &Delta;G&deg; (strongly favourable overall) corresponds mathematically to a large positive ln K, and therefore a large K, consistently describing the same product-favoured equilibrium."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: A student calculates K for a reaction from &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;4.38 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> at 298 K but forgets to convert kJ to J before using &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K. What is the main consequence of this error?",choices:["The calculated value of ln K (and hence K) will be far too small in magnitude, since R is in J, not kJ","The calculated value of K will have the wrong sign","The calculated value of K will be exactly 1000 times too large","There will be no effect, since units always cancel automatically"],explain:"Using &minus;4.38 instead of &minus;4380 in the equation makes the numerator a factor of 1000 too small, so the calculated ln K (and resulting K) would be far too small in magnitude; units must always be made consistent before substituting into &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Why does a K<sub>c</sub> value close to 1 (such as K<sub>c</sub> = 5.86 for the esterification reaction) correspond to only a modestly negative &Delta;G&deg;?",choices:["Because ln K is only a modest number when K is close to 1, so &minus;RT ln K also has a modest magnitude","Because K close to 1 always means &Delta;G&deg; is exactly zero","Because &Delta;G&deg; only depends on temperature, not on K","Because esterification reactions never have a defined &Delta;G&deg;"],explain:"When K is only moderately above 1 (such as 5.86), ln K is a relatively small positive number, so &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K is only modestly negative, consistent with an equilibrium mixture containing comparable amounts of reactants and products."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Which of the following best summarizes the relationship between K<sub>c</sub>, Q and &Delta;G&deg; developed in this sub-topic?",choices:["K<sub>c</sub> and &Delta;G&deg; both describe the fixed equilibrium position of a reaction at a given temperature, while Q describes the current, generally non-equilibrium, composition at any moment","K<sub>c</sub>, Q and &Delta;G&deg; are three unrelated quantities with no mathematical connection","Q is simply another name for K<sub>c</sub>, used interchangeably","&Delta;G&deg; changes continuously during a reaction in the same way that Q does"],explain:"K<sub>c</sub> and &Delta;G&deg; are linked by &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K and both describe the same fixed equilibrium position at a given temperature, whereas Q is calculated the same way as K<sub>c</sub> but using whatever concentrations happen to be present at a particular moment, which generally differ from the equilibrium values."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: A reaction is found to have Q equal to K<sub>c</sub> at a particular moment, while a temperature change is then applied. What happens next?",choices:["The mixture is no longer at equilibrium, since the temperature change alters K<sub>c</sub> itself, so the reaction shifts until Q again equals the new K<sub>c</sub>","Nothing changes, since Q = K<sub>c</sub> always remains true regardless of temperature","The reaction immediately stops occurring in both directions","Only Q changes, while K<sub>c</sub> remains exactly as it was before"],explain:"Because temperature changes the value of K<sub>c</sub> itself, a mixture that was at equilibrium (Q = K<sub>c</sub>) before the temperature change will no longer be at the new K<sub>c</sub>, so the reaction shifts until Q rises or falls to match the new K<sub>c</sub> value."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Which statement correctly combines the HL content on K<sub>c</sub> and temperature with Le Ch&acirc;telier's qualitative prediction for an exothermic reaction?",choices:["An increase in temperature shifts an exothermic equilibrium towards reactants (Le Ch&acirc;telier) and, quantitatively, decreases the value of K<sub>c</sub> for the same reaction","An increase in temperature shifts an exothermic equilibrium towards products and increases K<sub>c</sub>","Le Ch&acirc;telier's principle and the quantitative change in K<sub>c</sub> give contradictory predictions for an exothermic reaction","Temperature changes affect K<sub>c</sub> but never the qualitative equilibrium position predicted by Le Ch&acirc;telier's principle"],explain:"For an exothermic forward reaction, Le Ch&acirc;telier's principle predicts a shift towards reactants on heating, and this is exactly mirrored quantitatively by a decrease in the value of K<sub>c</sub> &ndash; the two descriptions are fully consistent."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: For a reaction with K<sub>c</sub> &asymp; 1, which statement about the equilibrium mixture and its K<sub>c</sub> units is generally true?",choices:["The mixture contains significant, comparable amounts of reactants and products; the numerical value of K<sub>c</sub> alone does not reveal this &ndash; the units must still be derived separately from the stoichiometry","The mixture is always entirely products, regardless of K<sub>c</sub>'s units","The mixture is always entirely reactants, regardless of K<sub>c</sub>'s units","K<sub>c</sub> &asymp; 1 only occurs for reactions with no units at all"],explain:"The magnitude of K<sub>c</sub> (close to 1) indicates comparable amounts of reactants and products regardless of its units, since the units of K<sub>c</sub> are determined purely by the reaction's stoichiometry, not by how far the reaction proceeds."},
{ch:18,q:"What is the difference between the equilibrium position of a reaction and its equilibrium constant, K<sub>c</sub>?",choices:["Equilibrium position describes the relative proportion of reactants and products once equilibrium is reached, and can shift with concentration or pressure; K<sub>c</sub> is a fixed numerical value at a given temperature that is unaffected by those changes","They are two names for exactly the same quantity, used interchangeably","Equilibrium position is temperature-independent, while K<sub>c</sub> always changes with concentration","K<sub>c</sub> describes equilibrium position only for heterogeneous reactions"],explain:"Equilibrium position is a qualitative or relative description of a mixture's composition, and can shift with concentration, pressure or temperature; K<sub>c</sub> is the precise numerical value of that composition's ratio, which only changes with temperature."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Why are catalysts never included in a K<sub>c</sub> expression?",choices:["Because a catalyst is not chemically changed by the reaction and has no effect on the equilibrium concentrations of reactants or products","Because catalysts always appear only in the denominator of K<sub>c</sub>","Because catalysts change K<sub>c</sub> so dramatically that including them would make calculations impossible","Because catalysts are classified as products, not reactants"],explain:"A catalyst speeds up the forward and backward reactions equally without being consumed, leaving the equilibrium concentrations of reactants and products (and hence K<sub>c</sub>) unaffected, so it is never written into the K<sub>c</sub> expression."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: What are the units of K<sub>c</sub> for the reverse of the Haber process, 2NH<sub>3</sub>(g) &harr; N<sub>2</sub>(g) + 3H<sub>2</sub>(g)?",choices:["mol<sup>2</sup> dm<sup>&minus;6</sup>","mol<sup>&minus;2</sup> dm<sup>6</sup>","No units","mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>"],explain:"K<sub>c</sub> = [N<sub>2</sub>][H<sub>2</sub>]<sup>3</sup> / [NH<sub>3</sub>]<sup>2</sup> has (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>)<sup>4</sup> over (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>)<sup>2</sup>, leaving (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>)<sup>2</sup>, equivalent to mol<sup>2</sup> dm<sup>&minus;6</sup> &ndash; the reciprocal of the units for the forward reaction, since reversing a reaction inverts its K<sub>c</sub> expression."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Why must state symbols always be included when writing an equation for which a K<sub>c</sub> expression will be derived?",choices:["To confirm that the reaction is homogeneous, since the K<sub>c</sub> expressions covered at this level only apply when all reactants and products are in the same phase","Because state symbols determine the sign of &Delta;H for the reaction","Because K<sub>c</sub> cannot be calculated at all without state symbols being present","Because state symbols indicate which species is the catalyst"],explain:"State symbols confirm whether a reaction is homogeneous (all species in the same phase); the simple K<sub>c</sub> expressions used in this topic apply only to homogeneous equilibria."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: For CO(g) + H<sub>2</sub>O(g) &harr; H<sub>2</sub>(g) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g), K<sub>c</sub> = 1.84 at 500&deg;C, with equilibrium [CO] = 0.150, [H<sub>2</sub>] = 0.200 and [CO<sub>2</sub>] = 0.0200 (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>). What is the equilibrium concentration of H<sub>2</sub>O?",choices:["0.0145 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","0.150 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","0.0200 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","0.0276 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>"],explain:"Rearranging K<sub>c</sub> = [H<sub>2</sub>][CO<sub>2</sub>] / ([CO][H<sub>2</sub>O]) gives [H<sub>2</sub>O] = [H<sub>2</sub>][CO<sub>2</sub>] / (K<sub>c</sub>[CO]) = (0.200 &times; 0.0200) / (1.84 &times; 0.150) = 0.00400 / 0.276 = 0.0145 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: For K<sub>c</sub> = 1.00 for CO(g) + H<sub>2</sub>O(g) &harr; CO<sub>2</sub>(g) + H<sub>2</sub>(g), suppose a mixture has [CO] = [H<sub>2</sub>O] = [CO<sub>2</sub>] = [H<sub>2</sub>] = 3.00 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>. What is Q, and is the mixture at equilibrium?",choices:["Q = 1.00, so the mixture is already at equilibrium, since Q = K<sub>c</sub>","Q = 3.00, so the reaction will shift to the left","Q = 0.333, so the reaction will shift to the right","Q cannot be calculated when all concentrations are equal"],explain:"Q = ([CO<sub>2</sub>][H<sub>2</sub>]) / ([CO][H<sub>2</sub>O]) = (3.00 &times; 3.00) / (3.00 &times; 3.00) = 1.00. Since Q equals K<sub>c</sub> (1.00), this mixture is already at equilibrium and no net reaction occurs."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: A reaction has &Delta;G&deg; = 0 at a particular temperature. What can be concluded about its equilibrium mixture?",choices:["K = 1, so the equilibrium mixture contains appreciable, comparable amounts of both reactants and products","The reaction does not occur at all at this temperature","K is undefined, since &Delta;G&deg; = 0 is not a physically possible value","The equilibrium mixture must be entirely products"],explain:"&Delta;G&deg; = 0 gives ln K = 0, so K = 1, meaning the reaction neither strongly favours products nor reactants &ndash; both are present in comparable, significant amounts at equilibrium."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: If the coefficients of a reaction equation are tripled, how does the new equilibrium constant relate to the original K<sub>c</sub>?",choices:["The new equilibrium constant is K<sub>c</sub><sup>3</sup>","The new equilibrium constant is 3K<sub>c</sub>","The new equilibrium constant is K<sub>c</sub> / 3","The new equilibrium constant is the cube root of K<sub>c</sub>"],explain:"Multiplying every coefficient in a reaction equation by a factor n raises the equilibrium constant to the power n; tripling the coefficients therefore cubes K<sub>c</sub>."},
{ch:18,q:"HL: Which of the following correctly identifies a reaction for which K<sub>c</sub> would have no units?",choices:["H<sub>2</sub>(g) + I<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; 2HI(g), since the total power of concentration in the numerator equals that in the denominator","N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub>(g) &harr; 2NO<sub>2</sub>(g), since this reaction increases the number of moles of gas","2SO<sub>2</sub>(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; 2SO<sub>3</sub>(g), since this reaction decreases the number of moles of gas","N<sub>2</sub>(g) + 3H<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; 2NH<sub>3</sub>(g), since ammonia is the only product"],explain:"K<sub>c</sub> has no units only when the sum of the coefficients of products equals the sum of the coefficients of reactants, as in H<sub>2</sub>(g) + I<sub>2</sub>(g) &harr; 2HI(g) (2 = 1 + 1); the other three reactions all have unequal totals, giving K<sub>c</sub> units that are a positive or negative power of mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>."}
]
